<section>
    <div class="container">
        <div class="row">
            <div class="col-lg-4">
                <div><img src="~/assets/img/dynamics365/Developer2.webp" class="img-fluid text-start ms-0 mb-5 me-3 p-3" alt="Supply Chain Management" /></div>
                <div><img src="~/assets/img/dynamics365/woman-tech-desk-chart-bureau.webp" class="img-fluid text-start ms-0 mb-5 mt-3 me-3 p-3" alt="Supply Chain Management" /></div>
            </div>
            <div class="col-lg-8 Imgborder" align="justify">
                <h3><b>Common Supply Chain Management Challenges and How to Address Them</b></h3>
                <p>Manufacturers may struggle with supplier delays, limited visibility, manual procurement, inconsistent communication, and disconnected logistics information.</p>
                <ul class="list-unstyled">
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i>  Supplier Delays: Use supplier performance information and alerts to identify potential delivery issues earlier.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i>  Limited Supply Chain Visibility: Centralize supplier, procurement, inventory, and logistics information.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i>  Manual Procurement: Automate purchase requests, approvals, purchase orders, and notifications.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i>  Inconsistent Supplier Communication: Use standardized workflows and communication processes.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i>  Disconnected Logistics: Integrate supply chain information with inventory, production, and logistics systems.</p>
                    </li>
                </ul><br />

                <h3><b>The Importance of Customization in Supply Chain Management</b></h3>
                <p>Supply chains differ by suppliers, materials, purchasing rules, locations, logistics processes, and business requirements. Customization supports these differences.</p>
                <ul class="list-unstyled">
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i>  Customize Procurement Workflows: Align purchase requests, approvals, supplier selection, and purchasing with business processes.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i>  Create Role-Based Workspaces: Provide procurement, warehouse, production, finance, and management users with relevant actions.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i>  Automate Supplier Activities: Configure supplier notifications, approvals, alerts, and follow-up workflows.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i>  Build Supply Chain Dashboards: Provide insights into supplier performance, lead times, purchasing, inventory, and delivery.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i>  Gather User Feedback: Continuously improve supply chain workflows using user and performance feedback.</p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

